    "body": "progress: `7de64cf` on `issue/103-turning-axial-rapids` (local, not pushed, `Refs #103`).\n\n- `turning_rapids.rs` now judges each axial rapid of a turning op in the meridian plane: the nose disc against the part + setup stock revolved about the fitted spindle axis, minus this op's own earlier feeds and the stock the program ahead faced off (#89's prior read, revolved). A hit is `RapidInStock` and blocks the post; the #96 exemption is gone.\n- Pinned by `turning_axial_rapids::an_axial_approach_inside_a_wider_bar_is_named_and_blocks_the_post` / `the_same_approach_beside_a_bar_the_parts_size_is_clean` + 3 unit tests.\n\nFinding: the law now convicts the 9 previously exempted tests plus `groove_and_part_post_to_the_lathe_golden`. Their fixtures declare a 40×40×40 box stock around a 12 mm bar while the turning kernels approach just outside the part (e.g. X13.7 Z25→Z2) - those rapids really are inside the declared stock. Decision (recorded in RECOMMENDED.md): the law is right and the fixtures are wrong - the fixtures get a bar-sized cylinder stock (NC goldens unchanged, stock isn't posted). The real gap - turning ops cannot rough from a bar wider than the part - goes to its own issue.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
    "pin": null,
